[9] The one place ERS leaves slack, and why it does not pay.
    ERS forces two level-0 supports to overlap in <= n_0/2, which is what
    makes the sign patterns of different supports unconstrained and gives the
    clean product A(n,n_0,n_0)*A(n_0,ceil(n_0/4)).  For n <= 44 and n_0 >= 30
    two n_0-subsets of [n] meet in >= 2n_0-n > n_0/2, so A(n,n_0,n_0) = 1 and
    level 0 is a SINGLE support.  One could instead take several supports with
    larger overlap and constrain the signs across them.  Worked out in the
    only case where it could matter, n = 44 with n_0 = 40:
      * two supports [44]\E_1, [44]\E_2 with E_1,E_2 disjoint 4-sets share
        36 coordinates, and <x,y> = 36 - 2*(disagreements there), so the only
        cross requirement is 'differ in >= 8 of 36' -- far weaker than the
        'differ in >= 10 of 40' required inside a single support;
      * but a 589824-word code projected to 36 coordinates saturates the cube:
        a Hamming ball of radius 7 in {0,1}^36 holds 10739176 points, and
        589824 * 10739176 / 2^36 = 92, i.e. an average point of {0,1}^36 already
        has about 92 first-support words within distance 7.  Essentially no
        room is left for a second support of comparable size;
      * and it would take 2097152/589824 = 3.56 supports' worth merely to match
        the single-support n_0 = 44 term that is already in use.
    At n = 44 the winning chain has n_0 = n, so there is exactly one support
    and no freedom of this kind at all.

